/* Psychology and Human Development, Human development. Original cards. */
(window.PA_CARDS = window.PA_CARDS || {}).psych = (window.PA_CARDS.psych || []).concat([
/* ---------- Development: Cognitive theories ---------- */
["Development: Cognitive theories","Piaget's key processes","In Piaget's theory, what is a schema?","A mental structure or framework that organises knowledge and guides action","Piaget saw cognitive growth as the building and refining of schemas. New experience is fitted to them or changes them."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Piaget's key processes","What does Piaget call fitting a new experience into an existing schema without changing it?","Assimilation","The child treats the new thing like something already known, for example sucking a new toy like a bottle."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Piaget's key processes","What does Piaget call changing an existing schema because a new experience will not fit it?","Accommodation","When assimilation fails, the schema itself must be altered or a new one created."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Piaget's key processes","What is Piaget's term for the balance reached when new information fits well with a child's schemas?","Equilibration","Imbalance (disequilibrium) drives assimilation and accommodation until a stable state is restored. This is the motor of development."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Piaget's key processes","What two processes together make up adaptation in Piaget's theory?","Assimilation and accommodation","Adaptation is how the child adjusts to the environment. The two processes work together to reach equilibrium."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Piaget's key processes","List Piaget's four stages of cognitive development in order with ages.","Sensorimotor (0-2), preoperational (2-7), concrete operational (7-11), formal operational (11 onwards)","Piaget held that the order is fixed and universal, though the age of each stage can vary."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Sensorimotor stage","In which Piagetian stage do infants first learn to tell themselves apart from the outside world?","Sensorimotor stage (birth to 2 years)","Infants know the world through senses and actions. Early self-recognition and object permanence emerge here."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Sensorimotor stage","What is object permanence?","Knowing that an object still exists when it cannot be seen","It shows the infant can hold a mental image of an absent object. It appears in the sensorimotor stage."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Sensorimotor stage","Around what age does object permanence begin to appear?","About 8 to 9 months","Before this, a hidden toy is treated as gone. It is fully established by about 18 to 24 months."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Sensorimotor stage","A 9-month-old boy laughs as his mother hides her face and then reappears. Which cognitive ability makes this game enjoyable?","Object permanence","Peek-a-boo works only once the baby expects the hidden face still to exist. It is the classic sensorimotor stage game."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Sensorimotor stage","What are circular reactions in Piaget's sensorimotor stage?","Repeating an action because it produced an interesting result","Primary reactions focus on the body, secondary on objects, and tertiary involve deliberate variation to explore."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Sensorimotor stage","At about what age do infants begin to use mental symbols and first words, ending the sensorimotor stage?","About 18 months","This symbolisation allows thinking without physical action and marks the move towards preoperational thought."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Sensorimotor stage","Is animism a feature of Piaget's sensorimotor stage?","No, it belongs to the preoperational stage","Animism needs symbolic thought, which comes after the sensorimotor stage."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Preoperational stage","What is centration in Piaget's theory?","Focusing on one striking feature of a problem and ignoring others","It explains why young children fail conservation tasks. They notice only the height of the water, not the width of the glass."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Preoperational stage","What is egocentrism in Piaget's sense?","Being unable to see the world from any viewpoint other than one's own","It is not selfishness. It is a cognitive limit of the preoperational stage."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Preoperational stage","Which Piaget experiment shows egocentrism?","The three mountains task","The child is asked what a doll sees from another side of a model. Preoperational children pick their own view."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Preoperational stage","A 4-year-old boy at a CAMHS assessment is shown a model with toys on a desk. He insists the examiner on the other side sees exactly what he sees. Which concept does this show?","Egocentrism","Preoperational children cannot take another person's visual perspective. This is a version of the three mountains task."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Preoperational stage","What is animism in child development?","Giving lifelike feelings and intentions to objects","For example, saying the chair is naughty for making them fall. It is typical of preoperational thinking."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Preoperational stage","What is irreversibility in Piaget's theory?","Being unable to mentally reverse an action back to its starting point","Preoperational children cannot imagine pouring the water back, so they fail conservation."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Preoperational stage","What is transductive reasoning?","Linking two specific things because they share a feature, without true logic","For example, cats and dogs both have four legs so they must be the same. It is neither inductive nor deductive."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Preoperational stage","A 3-year-old girl tells her mother that because she had a nap the sun has gone down, as it always does after her nap. What type of reasoning is this?","Transductive reasoning","She links two events from particular to particular. Preoperational children reason this way."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Preoperational stage","What is the semiotic (symbolic) function?","The ability to let one thing stand for another","It emerges in the preoperational stage and allows language, drawing, pretend play and deferred imitation."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Preoperational stage","A 4-year-old girl draws a tall stick figure for her dad and a small one for herself. Which Piagetian ability does this show?","The semiotic (symbolic) function","She uses marks on paper to represent people. This symbolic ability is a hallmark of the preoperational stage."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Concrete operational stage","What is conservation?","Knowing that quantity stays the same when shape or layout changes","It shows the child can reverse operations and attend to more than one dimension. It marks the concrete operational stage."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Concrete operational stage","A 9-year-old boy says a tall thin glass and a short wide glass hold the same water after watching it poured. Which Piagetian stage is he in?","Concrete operational","Conservation of liquid is achieved at about 7 years. He no longer centres on height alone."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Concrete operational stage","Which type of conservation is usually achieved last?","Conservation of volume (about 11 to 12 years)","Number comes first (about 6 to 7), then mass, then weight, then volume. Piaget called this staggered pattern horizontal decalage."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Concrete operational stage","What new logical skills appear in the concrete operational stage apart from conservation?","Seriation and classification","Children can order items by size and group them by shared features, but only with real, concrete objects."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Concrete operational stage","What is the main limit of thinking in the concrete operational stage?","It cannot handle abstract or hypothetical problems","Logic works only on real, perceivable objects and events. Abstract reasoning comes with formal operations."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Formal operational stage","Which Piagetian stage brings hypothetico-deductive reasoning?","Formal operational stage (from about 11 years)","The young person can form a hypothesis and test it in a planned way, and can think abstractly."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Formal operational stage","Which Piaget experiment tests formal operational thinking?","The pendulum task","The child must find what controls the swing speed by changing one variable at a time. This needs systematic hypothesis testing."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Formal operational stage","Do all adults reach Piaget's formal operational stage?","No, many adults never use formal operations consistently","Later research showed formal thinking depends on education and culture. It is a major criticism of Piaget."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Formal operational stage","Give two common criticisms of Piaget's theory.","He underestimated young children and gave too little weight to social and cultural factors","Simpler tasks show earlier competence, for example conservation and perspective-taking. Vygotsky stressed the social side."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Vygotsky and social learning","What is Vygotsky's zone of proximal development?","The gap between what a child can do alone and what they can do with skilled help","These are skills in the pipeline. Teaching is most useful when aimed inside this zone."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Vygotsky and social learning","What is scaffolding?","Temporary support from an adult that is withdrawn as the child gains skill","The term was introduced by Wood, Bruner and Ross. It is closely linked with Vygotsky's zone of proximal development."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Vygotsky and social learning","How does Vygotsky's view of the child differ from Piaget's?","Vygotsky sees the child as a social apprentice; Piaget sees a lone scientist","Vygotsky held that thinking develops through social interaction and culture, especially language."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Vygotsky and social learning","What did Vygotsky believe private speech (talking aloud to oneself) does?","It guides and regulates the child's own thinking and later becomes inner speech","Piaget saw it as egocentric speech that fades. Vygotsky saw it as a key tool of thought."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Theory of mind and self","In development, what does theory of mind allow a child to understand?","Understanding that oneself and others have thoughts, beliefs and wishes that guide behaviour","It lets us predict and explain what others do. It develops through early childhood."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Theory of mind and self","At what age do children usually pass false belief tasks?","About 4 years","At 3, children assume others know what they know. By 4 they understand another person can hold a wrong belief."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Theory of mind and self","Name a classic test of false belief.","The Sally-Anne test","Sally does not see her marble moved. Children who pass say Sally will look where she left it."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Theory of mind and self","The mother of a 6-year-old boy with autism asks a CAMHS doctor why he cannot tell how other children feel. Which core deficit best explains this?","Impaired theory of mind","Baron-Cohen called this mindblindness. Difficulty reading mental states limits empathy and social understanding."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Theory of mind and self","What does the rouge (mirror) test assess?","Visual self-recognition","A red mark is put on the nose. A child who touches their own nose in a mirror knows the image is themselves."],
["Development: Cognitive theories","Theory of mind and self","At what age do most children pass the rouge test?","About 18 to 24 months","Younger infants treat the reflection as another child. Self-recognition is needed for self-conscious emotions."],
/* ---------- Development: Psychodynamic theories ---------- */
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's models of the mind","Which Freudian model divides the mind into unconscious, preconscious and conscious?","The topographic model","Freud set it out in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900). It sorts mental events by how reachable they are by awareness."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's models of the mind","What three parts make up Freud's structural model of the mind?","Id, ego and superego","Described in The Ego and the Id (1923). It replaced the topographic model as Freud's main account."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's models of the mind","Which part of the mind works on the pleasure principle?","The id","The id seeks immediate drive gratification. The ego works on the reality principle."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's models of the mind","When is the superego formed, according to Freud?","On resolution of the Oedipus complex, about 5 to 6 years, as latency begins","Freud called the superego the heir to the Oedipus complex. The child takes in parental values and rules."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's models of the mind","What did Freud call the death instinct?","Thanatos","Freud set it against Eros, the life instinct, in his later drive theory."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's psychosexual stages","List Freud's psychosexual stages with ages.","Oral (0-18 months), anal (18 months-3 years), phallic (3-5 years), latency (5-6 to puberty), genital (puberty onwards)","Each stage centres libido on a different body zone. Problems at a stage can lead to fixation."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's psychosexual stages","In which Freudian stage does the Oedipus complex arise?","The phallic stage","The boy desires the mother and sees the father as a rival. Resolution comes through identifying with the father."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's psychosexual stages","With which psychosexual stage is castration anxiety linked?","The phallic stage","It is tied to the Oedipus complex and to discovering the difference between the sexes."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's psychosexual stages","What is the female version of the Oedipus complex called, and who named it?","The Electra complex, named by Carl Jung","Freud himself did not favour the term. In Greek myth Electra plots the death of her mother."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's psychosexual stages","Which Freudian stage is marked by quiet sexual drive and interest in learning and peers?","The latency stage","Drives are relatively dormant from about 5-6 years to puberty. Energy goes into school, play and friendships."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's psychosexual stages","Which Freudian stage centres on control, autonomy and toilet training?","The anal stage","The child strives for independence and control. Struggles here are linked with later obsessional traits."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's psychosexual stages","Which Freudian stage aims at mature object relations and adult identity?","The genital stage","From puberty, the person separates from parents and forms mature sexual and loving relationships."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's psychosexual stages","A 30-year-old man in an outpatient clinic is orderly, stubborn and very mean with money. In classic theory, at which stage is he fixated?","The anal stage","Orderliness, obstinacy and parsimony form the classic anal triad described by Freud."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's psychosexual stages","In classic psychoanalytic theory, excess drinking, smoking or overeating for comfort suggests fixation at which stage?","The oral stage","Too much or too little oral gratification in infancy was said to leave a lasting need for oral pleasure and dependence."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Freud's psychosexual stages","A 7-year-old boy has fled a war zone with his mother. What would Freud say is his psychosexual stage?","The latency stage","Latency runs from about 5-6 years to puberty, so a 7-year-old falls within it whatever his stress."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Defences in development","A 7-year-old boy who has just arrived in the UK as an asylum seeker starts sucking his thumb and clings to his mother. Which defence is this?","Regression","He returns to behaviour typical of an earlier stage to reduce anxiety. Regression is common in children under stress."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Defences in development","Which defence actively pushes painful thoughts and memories out of awareness?","Repression","It is unconscious forgetting that protects the mind. Freud saw it as the basic defence."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","How many psychosocial stages did Erikson describe and what span do they cover?","Eight, covering the whole life cycle from birth to death","Unlike Freud, Erikson continued development through adulthood and old age. Each stage is a crisis to be resolved."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","What is Erikson's first stage and its virtue?","Basic trust versus mistrust (birth to about 18 months); virtue hope","Reliable care lets the infant trust the world. It overlaps with Freud's oral stage."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","What is Erikson's second stage and its virtue?","Autonomy versus shame and doubt (about 18 months to 3 years); virtue will","The toddler wants to do things alone. It parallels Freud's anal stage."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","A 3-year-old boy insists on opening a heavy door himself and cries when his father tries to help. Which Erikson stage is he in?","Autonomy versus shame and doubt","He is asserting independence. Too much control or criticism at this stage leads to shame and doubt."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","What is Erikson's third stage and its virtue?","Initiative versus guilt (about 3 to 5 years); virtue purpose","The child plans and starts activities. Harsh responses breed guilt about acting on their own ideas."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","What is Erikson's fourth stage, its age range and its virtue?","Industry versus inferiority (about 5 to 13 years); virtue competence","The school-age child learns skills and takes pride in work. Repeated failure brings a sense of inferiority."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","A 7-year-old boy is upset because he keeps getting poor marks at school. Which Erikson crisis is he facing?","Industry versus inferiority","School achievement and mastery of skills are central to this stage."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","What is Erikson's fifth stage and its virtue?","Identity versus role confusion (adolescence, about 13 to 21); virtue fidelity","Puberty and social change push the young person to work out who they are and where they belong."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","A 15-year-old boy worries about whether to join a band at school and what his friends will think. Which Erikson stage applies?","Identity versus role confusion","Finding a group and a sense of self is the main task of adolescence in Erikson's model."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","What is Erikson's sixth stage and its virtue?","Intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood, about 21 to 40); virtue love","Having formed an identity, the person can risk close, committed relationships."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","What is Erikson's seventh stage and its virtue?","Generativity versus stagnation (about 40 to 60); virtue care","Concern for guiding the next generation, through children, work or community. Failure leads to self-absorption."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","A 50-year-old woman works extra shifts to save money so her children can leave home and start their lives. Which Erikson stage is she in?","Generativity versus stagnation","She is investing in the next generation, the central task of middle adulthood."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","What is Erikson's final stage and its virtue?","Ego integrity versus despair (about 60 onwards); virtue wisdom","The older person reviews their life. Acceptance gives integrity; regret gives despair and fear of death."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Erikson's psychosocial stages","A 72-year-old retired man reads, travels and does a little part-time work, and feels content with how his life went. Which Erikson stage is this?","Ego integrity versus despair","He accepts his one life cycle as it was. This is the healthy outcome of Erikson's last stage."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Mahler's separation-individuation","What did Margaret Mahler's theory describe?","How an infant gains a sense of self separate from the mother","She described phases from birth to about 3-5 years, based on observing mothers and infants."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Mahler's separation-individuation","What is Mahler's first phase and its age?","Normal autism, birth to about 2 months","The infant sleeps more than it is awake, in a state like intrauterine life, with little awareness of others. Mahler later dropped this phase."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Mahler's separation-individuation","What is Mahler's symbiotic phase?","About 2 to 5 months; infant and mother are felt as one fused unit","The infant is aware of the mother but has no sense of being a separate individual."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Mahler's separation-individuation","A 4-month-old girl responds to her mother but shows no sense of being a separate person. Which Mahler phase is she in?","The symbiotic phase","Mother and baby are experienced as a single system at this age."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Mahler's separation-individuation","When does Mahler's separation-individuation phase begin and end?","From about 5 months to about 3-5 years","It has four subphases: differentiation, practising, rapprochement and object constancy."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Mahler's separation-individuation","What happens in Mahler's differentiation subphase?","About 5 to 10 months; the infant begins to notice mother as different from self","Hatching occurs as the infant becomes more alert to the outside world."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Mahler's separation-individuation","What happens in Mahler's practising subphase?","About 10 to 18 months; the toddler eagerly explores, helped by walking","The child has a love affair with the world, checking back with mother as a base."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Mahler's separation-individuation","An 18-month-old girl explores alone but keeps checking where her mother is and runs back for comfort. Which Mahler subphase is this?","Rapprochement (about 18 to 24 months)","She swings between wanting independence and needing closeness, as she realises her separateness."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Mahler's separation-individuation","What is achieved in Mahler's object constancy subphase?","A stable inner image of the mother, so the child copes when she is away","From about the third year, the child knows mother will return and can function on their own."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Klein and object relations","Who is most closely linked with founding object relations theory?","Melanie Klein","She focused on the infant's early relationships with objects (people and part-people) rather than on drives alone."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Klein and object relations","What does object relations theory say is the main motivating drive?","Seeking relationships with others","Classic Freudian theory stressed pleasure-seeking drives. Object relations theorists see relating as primary."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Klein and object relations","In Klein's theory, what happens in the paranoid-schizoid position?","The infant splits objects into wholly good and wholly bad","Good and bad are kept apart by splitting and projection, with fears of persecution. It covers roughly the first few months."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Klein and object relations","What conflict is mastered in Klein's depressive position?","Ambivalence: love and hate for the same whole person","The infant realises the good and bad mother are one, and feels guilt and a wish to repair. It follows the paranoid-schizoid position."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Klein and object relations","With which theorist is splitting as an early defence most associated?","Melanie Klein","She saw splitting and projective identification as the first defences of the paranoid-schizoid position."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Winnicott","Who described good-enough mothering?","Donald Winnicott","The mother need not be perfect. Responsive care that fails in small, bearable ways helps the infant grow."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Winnicott","What is a transitional object?","A comfort object, such as a blanket or teddy, that stands in for the mother","Winnicott described it. It helps the infant cope with separation and becoming independent."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Winnicott","What is the main function of a transitional object?","It reduces anxiety, especially at times of stress or separation, such as bedtime","It gives a soothing sense of security when the mother is absent."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Winnicott","Name three concepts linked with Winnicott.","Holding environment, potential space, true and false self","Also the transitional object, good-enough mother and primary maternal preoccupation. Object permanence is Piaget's, not his."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Jung and Adler","Who introduced the terms introversion and extraversion?","Carl Jung","They are part of Jung's analytic psychology, along with archetypes and the collective unconscious."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Jung and Adler","What is the collective unconscious in Jung's theory?","A shared, inherited layer of the mind holding archetypes","Archetypes are universal images such as the mother, hero and shadow."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Jung and Adler","What did Jung mean by individuation?","The lifelong process of becoming a whole, integrated self","Jung saw midlife as a key time for this, when the person attends to neglected parts of the self."],
["Development: Psychodynamic theories","Jung and Adler","Which theorist is linked with sibling rivalry, birth order and the inferiority complex?","Alfred Adler","Adler's individual psychology stressed striving to overcome feelings of inferiority."],
/* ---------- Development: Attachment ---------- */
["Development: Attachment","Bowlby's theory","Who founded attachment theory?","John Bowlby","He drew on ethology and psychoanalysis. He argued infants have an inborn need to attach to a caregiver."],
["Development: Attachment","Bowlby's theory","Why did Bowlby think attachment evolved?","Human infants are helpless and would die without a protective caregiver","Staying close to the carer improves survival. Crying and smiling act as signals that bring the carer near."],
["Development: Attachment","Bowlby's theory","What did Bowlby mean by monotropy?","The tendency to form one main attachment, usually to the mother","He accepted other attachments exist, but said one is primary. Later research found multiple attachments are the rule."],
["Development: Attachment","Bowlby's theory","Must the primary attachment figure be the mother?","No, it is the most responsive carer, whoever that is","Attachment forms with the person who responds sensitively, not simply who feeds or gives birth."],
["Development: Attachment","Bowlby's theory","What is an internal working model?","A mental template of self and others built from early attachment","It shapes expectations of relationships into adulthood. Bowlby introduced the term."],
["Development: Attachment","Bowlby's theory","What is Bowlby's maternal deprivation hypothesis?","Prolonged separation from the mother in early years harms emotional and social development","He proposed a critical period in about the first 2 to 3 years. Later work softened this view."],
["Development: Attachment","Bowlby's theory","In Bowlby's 44 juvenile thieves study, what was linked with affectionless psychopathy?","Prolonged early separation from the mother","Most thieves rated as affectionless had long early separations. The study was retrospective, so causation is unclear."],
["Development: Attachment","Bowlby's theory","Does attachment behaviour stop at puberty?","No, it continues throughout life","Adults still seek closeness to attachment figures, especially when stressed or ill."],
["Development: Attachment","Phases of attachment","When is the most sensitive period for forming attachment in humans?","About 6 to 18 months","Clear attachment forms in the second half of the first year. Disruption after this is more harmful."],
["Development: Attachment","Phases of attachment","At what age does attachment behaviour usually peak?","About 12 to 18 months","Proximity-seeking and protest at separation are strongest at this age, then lessen as the child grows."],
["Development: Attachment","Phases of attachment","What happens in the pre-attachment phase (birth to about 8-12 weeks)?","The baby orients to people, including the mother, without discrimination","The baby tracks faces and responds to voices but does not yet prefer one person."],
["Development: Attachment","Phases of attachment","A 5-month-old girl lets strangers hold her happily but also smiles most at her mother and grandmother. Which attachment phase is she in?","Attachment in the making (indiscriminate attachment)","From about 8-12 weeks to 6 months, the baby starts to prefer familiar people but still accepts strangers."],
["Development: Attachment","Phases of attachment","At what age does an infant first show a clear, preferential attachment with protest at separation?","About 7 to 9 months","This clear-cut phase brings separation protest and stranger wariness."],
["Development: Attachment","Phases of attachment","What is a goal-corrected partnership in attachment?","From about 2-3 years, the child takes account of the carer's plans and goals","With language and theory of mind, the child can negotiate separations rather than just protest."],
["Development: Attachment","Phases of attachment","By what stage of childhood does attachment behaviour lessen enough for a carer to be substituted more easily?","School age","Older children carry an inner model of the carer and tolerate separations better."],
["Development: Attachment","Phases of attachment","Who studied the order in which Glasgow infants formed attachments?","Schaffer and Emerson (1964)","They followed 60 infants over the first 18 months, noting who they protested at being parted from."],
["Development: Attachment","Phases of attachment","What did Schaffer and Emerson find about multiple attachments by 18 months?","Most infants (about 87%) had more than one attachment","Multiple attachments were the norm. The main one was with whoever responded most, not who fed most."],
["Development: Attachment","Phases of attachment","A 20-month-old girl cries when her mother, her father or her childminder leaves. What term describes having several attachment figures?","Multiple attachments (polytropy)","Attachment to several carers is normal. It challenges Bowlby's strong idea of monotropy."],
["Development: Attachment","Separation responses","At around what age do babies begin to show stranger anxiety?","About 7 to 9 months","It needs the baby to recognise familiar faces. It peaks around the end of the first year and fades after about 2-3 years."],
["Development: Attachment","Separation responses","Three babies aged 3, 5 and 12 months are with their mothers in a GP waiting room. When the mothers step out, which baby is most likely to cry?","The 12-month-old","Separation anxiety starts after clear attachment forms at about 7-9 months. Younger babies do not yet protest."],
["Development: Attachment","Separation responses","At what age is separation anxiety most common in typical development?","About 10 to 18 months","It usually fades by the end of the third year as object constancy develops."],
["Development: Attachment","Separation responses","What three stages follow when a young child is separated from the attachment figure?","Protest, despair, detachment","Described by Bowlby and the Robertsons. The first reaction is angry crying and searching."],
["Development: Attachment","Separation responses","What did Rene Spitz call the depression of infants separated from their mothers in institutions?","Anaclitic depression (hospitalism)","Infants became withdrawn, unresponsive and prone to illness. They recovered if mothering returned."],
["Development: Attachment","Separation responses","What is the secure base effect?","The child uses the attachment figure as a safe base from which to explore","Ainsworth described it. Exploration falls if the carer leaves."],
["Development: Attachment","Separation responses","A 3-year-old boy at a toddler group wanders off to play with others but checks back to his mother now and then. What is this called?","The secure base effect","The present carer gives the confidence to explore. Proximity-seeking returns if he becomes frightened."],
["Development: Attachment","Separation responses","A 12-month-old girl follows her mother around the room and tries to stay close to her. What is this attachment behaviour called?","Proximity seeking","Keeping close to the carer is the core attachment behaviour. It increases under threat."],
["Development: Attachment","Strange Situation","Who devised the Strange Situation procedure?","Mary Ainsworth","It uses a series of brief separations and reunions with the carer and a stranger to classify attachment."],
["Development: Attachment","Strange Situation","For what age is the Strange Situation designed?","About 12 to 18 months","At this age attachment is clearly formed and separation responses are easy to see."],
["Development: Attachment","Strange Situation","In the Strange Situation, which episode gives most information about attachment type?","Reunion with the caregiver","How the infant greets and is comforted by the carer shows the quality of attachment best."],
["Development: Attachment","Strange Situation","What does a securely attached infant do in the Strange Situation?","Is upset at separation but is quickly comforted on reunion and returns to play","The carer works as a secure base. This is linked with sensitive, attuned parenting."],
["Development: Attachment","Strange Situation","What is the most common attachment type in Western samples, and roughly how common?","Secure, about 60 to 70%","Avoidant is about 15-20%, ambivalent about 10-15% and disorganised about 5-10%."],
["Development: Attachment","Strange Situation","Which attachment type shows little distress at separation and ignores the carer on reunion?","Insecure-avoidant","The child seems unconcerned and treats the stranger and mother much the same. It is linked with unresponsive, rejecting care."],
["Development: Attachment","Strange Situation","A 2-year-old boy keeps playing when his mother leaves and ignores her when she returns. What attachment type is likely?","Insecure-avoidant","Minimal distress and active avoidance on reunion define this pattern."],
["Development: Attachment","Strange Situation","Which attachment type shows great distress at separation but anger and resistance to comfort on reunion?","Insecure-ambivalent (resistant)","The child may seek contact then push away and takes long to settle. It is linked with inconsistent care."],
["Development: Attachment","Strange Situation","A 14-month-old girl is distraught when her mother leaves. On her return she reaches to be held but then pushes her away angrily and keeps crying. What attachment type is this?","Insecure-ambivalent (resistant)","Seeking and resisting comfort at once is the hallmark of this pattern."],
["Development: Attachment","Strange Situation","Who added the disorganised attachment category?","Main and Solomon","Some infants did not fit Ainsworth's three groups, showing odd or contradictory behaviour."],
["Development: Attachment","Strange Situation","A 13-month-old boy freezes and stays rigid when his mother returns and does not seek contact. What attachment type is this?","Disorganised attachment","Freezing, confused or contradictory behaviour on reunion shows no coherent strategy for coping."],
["Development: Attachment","Attachment and parenting","Which parenting style is linked with insecure-avoidant attachment?","Unresponsive or rejecting care","The infant learns that showing distress does not bring comfort, so it stops showing it."],
["Development: Attachment","Attachment and parenting","Which parenting style is linked with insecure-ambivalent attachment?","Inconsistent care","The infant cannot predict the carer's response, so it heightens demands for attention."],
["Development: Attachment","Attachment and parenting","What caregiving is linked with disorganised attachment?","Maltreatment, or frightened or frightening care, often with unresolved parental trauma","The carer is both the source of fear and the source of comfort, so the infant cannot organise a response."],
["Development: Attachment","Adult Attachment Interview","Who developed the Adult Attachment Interview?","Mary Main and colleagues (George, Kaplan and Main)","It is an hour-long, semi-structured interview about childhood relationships with carers."],
["Development: Attachment","Adult Attachment Interview","What is the Adult Attachment Interview mainly scored on?","The coherence of how the person talks about childhood","It is not scored on what happened or on attachment to a specific person, but on how the story is organised."],
["Development: Attachment","Adult Attachment Interview","Name the four Adult Attachment Interview classifications.","Autonomous (secure), dismissing, preoccupied (entangled), unresolved (disorganised)","Each roughly matches an infant Strange Situation category."],
["Development: Attachment","Adult Attachment Interview","In the Adult Attachment Interview, a 35-year-old woman speaks freely and coherently about both good and bad childhood experiences. How is she classified?","Autonomous (secure)","Coherent, balanced talk about the past marks a secure state of mind, whatever the past was like."],
["Development: Attachment","Adult Attachment Interview","In the Adult Attachment Interview, a 40-year-old man gives brief answers, idealises his parents and often says he cannot remember. How is he classified?","Dismissing","Minimising attachment and poor recall are typical. It matches infant avoidant attachment."],
["Development: Attachment","Adult Attachment Interview","In the Adult Attachment Interview, a 29-year-old woman talks at length about her childhood and becomes angry and tearful. How is she classified?","Preoccupied (entangled)","She is still caught up in past relationships. It matches infant ambivalent attachment."],
["Development: Attachment","Adult Attachment Interview","Does a parent's Adult Attachment Interview category predict their infant's attachment?","Yes, it predicts the infant's Strange Situation category well","This intergenerational transmission is thought to work partly through parental sensitivity."],
["Development: Attachment","Animal studies and imprinting","What did Harlow's rhesus monkey studies show?","Infant monkeys clung to a soft cloth mother, not a wire mother that gave milk","Contact comfort mattered more than food. This undermined the idea that attachment is driven by feeding."],
["Development: Attachment","Animal studies and imprinting","What is imprinting?","Rapid learning in a critical period after birth in which a young animal follows the first moving object it sees","Lorenz showed it in geese. It is quick, hard to reverse and limited to a critical period."],
["Development: Attachment","Animal studies and imprinting","Who is best known for demonstrating imprinting in goslings?","Konrad Lorenz","Goslings that saw him first after hatching followed him as if he were their mother."],
["Development: Attachment","Attachment and psychopathology","Is secure attachment protective against childhood disorders?","Yes, secure attachment is a protective factor; insecure attachment is a risk factor","Insecure attachment is linked with later behaviour and emotional problems, but is not a disorder itself."],
["Development: Attachment","Attachment and psychopathology","Insecure attachment in early childhood is most clearly linked with which behavioural disorder?","Oppositional defiant disorder (externalising problems)","Disorganised attachment in particular predicts later aggression and conduct problems."],
["Development: Attachment","Attachment and psychopathology","Which personality disorder is most strongly linked with childhood abuse and early separation from carers?","Borderline personality disorder","A large majority report physical or sexual abuse or long early separations. Insecure attachment is a known risk factor."],
/* ---------- Development: Temperament and parenting ---------- */
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Thomas and Chess","What is temperament?","Inborn, biologically based style of behaving and reacting","Thomas and Chess called it the how of behaviour, as opposed to the why (motive) or the what (content)."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Thomas and Chess","What study did Thomas and Chess run on temperament?","The New York Longitudinal Study","They followed about 140 children from infancy into adulthood using parental interviews."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Thomas and Chess","How many temperament dimensions did Thomas and Chess describe?","Nine","Activity, rhythmicity, approach-withdrawal, adaptability, intensity, threshold, mood, distractibility, and attention span with persistence."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Thomas and Chess","Name the three temperament types of Thomas and Chess with their rough frequencies.","Easy (about 40%), slow-to-warm-up (about 15%), difficult (about 10%)","About a third of children did not fit any of the three types."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Thomas and Chess","What are the features of an easy temperament?","Regular routines, positive approach to new things, quick adaptation, mild positive mood","These children are easy to care for and settle quickly."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Thomas and Chess","An 18-month-old girl sleeps and feeds at regular times, settles quickly after crying and smiles at strangers. What is her temperament type?","Easy","Regular rhythms, positive approach and quick soothing are typical of the easy child."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Thomas and Chess","What are the features of a difficult temperament?","Irregular routines, withdrawal from new things, slow adaptation, intense negative mood","Fiery emotional outbursts are typical. These children are at higher risk of later behaviour problems."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Thomas and Chess","What are the features of a slow-to-warm-up temperament?","Mild withdrawal from new things, slow adaptation, low intensity and low activity","Unlike the difficult child, emotions are mild, not fiery. They adjust given time."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Thomas and Chess","A 2-year-old boy at nursery clings at first and quietly watches for days before he joins in, without tantrums. What is his temperament type?","Slow-to-warm-up","Gradual, mild adaptation to new settings without intense emotion fits this type."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Thomas and Chess","What did Thomas and Chess mean by goodness of fit?","The match between a child's temperament and the demands of the environment","A good match gives better outcomes. Child and parent shape each other's behaviour in both directions."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Other temperament models","What does EAS stand for in Buss and Plomin's model?","Emotionality, activity, sociability","Their first model also had impulsivity. Attention span was not one of their dimensions."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Other temperament models","Are Buss and Plomin's EAS traits stable over time?","Yes, they appear early, are heritable and are fairly stable","They were chosen because they show early onset, a genetic basis and stability across development."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Other temperament models","What is Kagan's behavioural inhibition?","A temperament of shyness and withdrawal from unfamiliar people and situations","About 15% of toddlers show it. It raises the risk of later anxiety disorders, especially social anxiety."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Parenting styles","Who described the main parenting styles based on warmth and control?","Diana Baumrind","She described authoritative, authoritarian and permissive styles. Maccoby and Martin later added neglectful."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Parenting styles","Which parenting style combines high warmth with clear, firm limits?","Authoritative","It is linked with the best outcomes: good self-esteem, social skill and school performance."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Parenting styles","Which parenting style combines high control with low warmth?","Authoritarian","Rules are strict and not explained. Children tend to be obedient but anxious and lower in self-esteem."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Parenting styles","Parents of a 10-year-old boy set almost no rules, avoid conflict and give in to most demands, though they are loving. What parenting style is this?","Permissive","High warmth with low control. Children may show poor self-control and impulsivity."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Parenting styles","Which parenting style is linked with the poorest child outcomes?","Neglectful (uninvolved): low warmth and low control","Lack of both support and supervision is linked with behaviour problems, substance use and poor attainment."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Parenting styles","What are restricted and elaborated language codes?","Bernstein's two styles of speech: short, context-bound speech versus longer, explicit, abstract speech","Restricted code uses short, simple sentences about the here and now. Elaborated code allows abstract and past or future ideas."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Parenting styles","Which features mark Bernstein's elaborated language code?","Long, complex sentences that do not depend on context, with abstract ideas","Bernstein linked it with middle-class families and school success. Restricted code relies on shared context."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Family factors","Which children are most affected by family instability?","Boys, younger children and those with difficult or hyperactive temperament","These children are more vulnerable to stress at home."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Family factors","What is the most common type of problem in children exposed to family instability?","Behavioural difficulties","It also harms cognitive and school achievement. Psychosis is not a typical outcome."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Family factors","What are the common effects of parental separation on children?","Behaviour problems, lower school achievement, low self-esteem and physical complaints","Effects can occur at any age, even in infants. Better motivation for school is not an effect."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Family factors","What helps children adapt well after parental divorce?","Adequate money, good contact with the non-resident parent, low conflict and a stable new family","Most children adapt well if these are in place. Ongoing parental conflict is the most harmful factor."],
["Development: Temperament and parenting","Family factors","Which sex tends to be more affected in the short term by parental divorce?","Boys","Boys more often show externalising problems after divorce. Girls are not more affected."],
/* ---------- Development: Milestones, language and play ---------- */
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Social and emotional milestones","At what age does the first social smile appear?","About 6 weeks","Earlier smiles are reflex. A smile in response to a face is a key early social milestone."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Social and emotional milestones","At what age does an infant start to tell familiar from unfamiliar faces?","About 3 months","This is a step towards preferential attachment, which follows a few months later."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Social and emotional milestones","Which emotions are present in the first 6 months of life?","Primary emotions: joy, sadness, anger, fear, disgust and surprise","These basic emotions do not need a sense of self. They develop further over the first three years."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Social and emotional milestones","When do self-conscious emotions such as guilt and shame appear?","After about 2 years, becoming clear by about 3","They need a sense of self, which arrives with self-recognition at about 18-24 months."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Social and emotional milestones","A 4-year-old girl talks to an imaginary friend at home. Her parents bring her to the GP worried. What is the best response?","Reassure them that this is normal at her age","Imaginary companions are common between about 3 and 10 years. They are not a sign of psychosis."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Social and emotional milestones","Can a 5-year-old usually tell the time to the nearest quarter hour?","No; this usually comes at about 6 to 7 years","Children read the hour at about 5, the half hour at about 6, and the quarter hour at about 7."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Motor and drawing milestones","At what age do most babies sit without support?","About 6 to 8 months","Not sitting unsupported by about 9 months is a red flag for motor delay."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Motor and drawing milestones","At what age do most children walk alone?","About 12 months (range about 10 to 18 months)","Not walking by 18 months needs assessment."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Motor and drawing milestones","At what age does a mature pincer grip develop?","About 10 to 12 months","An early pincer grip appears at about 9 months. It allows picking up small objects."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Motor and drawing milestones","Why is clear hand preference before about 12 months a concern?","It may point to weakness of the other arm, such as cerebral palsy","Handedness usually settles from about 2 to 3 years. Early preference suggests a one-sided problem."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Motor and drawing milestones","At what age can a child build a tower of 6 cubes?","About 2 years","A tower of 9 cubes comes at about 3 years."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Motor and drawing milestones","At about what age can a child build a tower of 9 cubes, copy a circle and walk upstairs one foot per step?","About 3 years","These are typical 3-year milestones. Coming down, they still use two feet per step."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Motor and drawing milestones","Give the usual ages for copying a circle, cross, square, triangle and diamond.","Circle 3, cross 4, square about 4 to 4.5, triangle 5, diamond 6 years","Copying shapes needs fine motor and visual-spatial skill and is a useful non-verbal developmental check."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Motor and drawing milestones","At about what age can a child copy a cross but not a triangle, and is dry by day and night?","About 4 years","Copying a cross and full bladder and bowel control both fit age 4. Triangles come at about 5."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Motor and drawing milestones","At what age can a child usually hop on one foot?","About 4 years","Standing briefly on one foot comes at 3. Skipping follows at about 5."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Motor and drawing milestones","How can children's drawings of a person be used clinically?","As a non-verbal estimate of cognitive development","The Goodenough draw-a-person approach scores detail. Between 3 and 6 years drawings grow from a simple head to a full figure."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Perceptual development","At birth, which sense is the least mature?","Vision","There is little visual experience in the womb. Newborn acuity is very poor, about 20/300 to 20/400."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Perceptual development","By what age does visual acuity reach near-adult levels?","About 6 months","Acuity improves fast over the first months as the visual cortex and retina mature."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Perceptual development","At what distance do newborns see faces most clearly?","About 20 to 30 cm","This is about the distance between a feeding baby and the mother's face."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Perceptual development","What visual abilities are present at birth?","Tracking and scanning objects, telling brightness apart and fixing on objects","Newborns also prefer face-like patterns, though fine detail is not seen."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Perceptual development","At about what age do infants develop colour vision and accommodation?","About 4 months","Before this, focus is fixed. Depth cues and colour then mature quickly."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Perceptual development","What does the visual cliff test?","Depth perception in infants","Gibson and Walk used a glass sheet over a drop. Crawling infants would not cross the apparent cliff."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","At what age does cooing begin?","About 6 to 8 weeks","Vowel-like sounds come first. Consonant babbling follows a few months later."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","At what age does babbling usually begin?","About 6 months","Repeated syllables such as 'bababa' appear by 6 to 9 months. Lack of babbling by about 10 months needs a hearing check."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","At what age do children usually say their first words such as 'mama' or 'dada' with meaning?","About 12 months","One-word speech runs from about 10 to 18 months."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","About how many words does a typical 18-month-old use?","Roughly 6 to 40 words","A vocabulary spurt usually follows between 18 and 24 months."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","What is telegraphic speech?","Short phrases of key words without linking words, such as 'Mummy cup'","It appears at about 18 to 24 months. Small words like prepositions and articles are left out."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","An 18-month-old boy points to a mug and says 'Mummy cup'. Which stage of language is he at?","The two-word (telegraphic) stage","He joins meaningful words without grammar words. This is typical around 18 to 24 months."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","About how many words does a 2-year-old usually have?","More than 200 to 250 words","By 2 years children join words in pairs or short phrases. Fewer than 50 words at 2 is a common red flag for delay."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","At what age do children start using short sentences with grammar words such as 'the' and 'can'?","About 3 years","This marks early grasp of syntax. Speech becomes understandable to strangers most of the time."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","By what age do children usually use grammar mostly correctly?","About 4 years","Mistakes of over-regularising, such as 'goed', are common and show rule learning."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","By what age does a child usually speak in full adult-like sentences and tell stories?","About 5 years","By 5 speech is fully intelligible and close to adult form."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","Which children are more likely to show slow language development?","Boys, twins, children in large families, lower social class and those lacking stimulation","Less one-to-one adult talk and hearing loss are key reasons. Deaf and neglected children are at risk."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language milestones","What is 'motherese' (infant-directed speech)?","Short, simple, high-pitched and repetitive speech used with infants","It holds attention and helps infants pick out words and sounds."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language theories","Who proposed the language acquisition device?","Noam Chomsky","He argued children are born with a capacity for grammar, explaining fast learning from limited input."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language theories","Which theorist explained language learning through reinforcement?","B F Skinner","He saw language as operant behaviour shaped by reward. It cannot explain new sentences children invent."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language theories","What did Bruner propose to complement Chomsky's language acquisition device?","The language acquisition support system (adult support during shared routines)","Adults scaffold language in games and daily routines, a social view close to Vygotsky's."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Language theories","What is the critical period hypothesis for language?","Language is learned most easily before puberty","Children deprived of language early, such as Genie, never gained full grammar."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Play","Give the typical sequence of social play in early childhood.","Solitary, then parallel (about 2), associative (about 3), cooperative (about 3-4), then rule-governed (about 5-7)","Parten described these stages. Play becomes more social and structured with age."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Play","What type of play is a 6-month-old shaking a rattle?","Sensorimotor play","It explores the properties of objects through action. It is the earliest form of play."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Play","What type of play is a 2-year-old speaking to his granddad on a toy phone?","Symbolic (pretend) play","One object stands for another or for something absent. It is clear by about 2 years."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Play","What is parallel play?","Playing alongside other children with similar toys but without interacting","It is typical at about 2 years."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Play","A 4-year-old girl in a GP waiting room offers to share her toy and plays a game with another child. What type of play is this?","Cooperative play","Sharing, turn-taking and joint goals mark cooperative play, usual from about 3 to 4 years."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Play","A 7-year-old boy enjoys board games with friends his own age. What type of play is this?","Rule-governed play","Games with set rules and competition start at about 5 and grow through middle childhood."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Developmental fears","Which fears are typical of infancy?","Loud noises, falling, sudden movement and strangers","These need little learning and fade with age."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Developmental fears","At about what age do fears of animals peak?","About 3 years","Most animal fears in pre-school children fade without treatment."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Developmental fears","At what age does fear of the dark usually appear?","About 4 to 5 years","It is linked with growing imagination. Fear of imaginary creatures follows from about 5 onwards."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Developmental fears","When do fears of illness, failure and death usually emerge?","From the teenage years onwards","They need abstract thought about the future and one's own mortality."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Developmental fears","Which fears show no clear age trend?","Fear of snakes and of natural events such as storms","They seem to appear at any age and may have an evolutionary basis."],
["Development: Milestones, language and play","Developmental fears","When does fear of peer rejection peak?","About 15 years","It rises from about 11 to 13, peaks around 15 and falls by 17. It is stronger in girls."],
/* ---------- Development: Adversity and deprivation ---------- */
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Deprivation and privation","How did Rutter distinguish deprivation from privation?","Deprivation is loss of or damage to an attachment; privation is never forming one","Rutter argued privation has worse effects, such as affectionless psychopathy and developmental delay."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Deprivation and privation","What book did Rutter write that challenged Bowlby's maternal deprivation idea?","Maternal Deprivation Reassessed (1972)","He showed that family discord and lack of any bond, not separation alone, drive poor outcomes."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Deprivation and privation","What did the English and Romanian Adoptees study find about the length of early deprivation?","Longer deprivation was linked with more lasting problems, in a dose-response way","Children adopted before about 6 months did best. Later adoption was linked with persisting difficulties."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Deprivation and privation","Name the deprivation-specific problems seen in children adopted from Romanian institutions.","Quasi-autism, disinhibited attachment, inattention and overactivity, and cognitive impairment","These were linked with institutional care lasting beyond about 6 months."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Deprivation and privation","What did the Bucharest Early Intervention Project show?","Moving institutionalised children into foster care improved cognitive recovery, especially if done early","It was a randomised trial. Earlier placement gave greater gains, pointing to a sensitive period."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Deprivation and privation","What did Tizard and Hodges find in children adopted after long institutional care?","Most formed attachments with adoptive parents by 8, but at 16 still had peer problems and sought attention","These lasting social problems in adolescence are called ex-institutional syndrome."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Deprivation and privation","Before what age does adoption give a good chance of normal attachment?","Before about 4 to 5 years, and the earlier the better","Earlier adoption gives more chance to form new secure attachments."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Deprivation and privation","A 6-year-old girl adopted from an overseas orphanage goes off happily with strangers and hugs unfamiliar adults at the GP surgery. What ICD-11 diagnosis should be considered?","Disinhibited social engagement disorder","Overfamiliar, indiscriminate behaviour with strangers follows severely neglectful early care."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Deprivation and privation","How does reactive attachment disorder differ from disinhibited social engagement disorder?","Reactive attachment disorder is withdrawn and does not seek comfort; disinhibited is overfamiliar with strangers","Both follow grossly inadequate early care, but show opposite social patterns."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Risk and resilience","What did the Cambridge Study in Delinquent Development follow?","About 400 boys from South London from age 8 into adulthood","Led by West and later Farrington. It looked for childhood predictors of offending."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Risk and resilience","Name the main childhood predictors of later offending in the Cambridge Study.","Troublesome behaviour, impulsivity, low IQ and attainment, family criminality, poverty and poor parenting","Measured at 8 to 10 years, these predicted delinquency and adult offending."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Risk and resilience","What did Rutter's Isle of Wight study show about family adversity factors?","Risk of child disorder rose sharply when two or more factors were present","One factor alone added little. Risk multiplies rather than adds."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Risk and resilience","Name factors that protect a family against child abuse.","A supportive network of relatives or friends and a stable, supportive partner","Parents abused as children are less likely to abuse if they have worked through their past and have support."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Risk and resilience","What are adverse childhood experiences (ACEs)?","Abuse, neglect and household problems such as violence, parental mental illness, substance misuse or prison","More ACEs give a graded rise in later mental and physical illness."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Risk and resilience","What is resilience in child development?","Doing well despite significant adversity","It comes from the child, the family and the wider community, for example one warm, reliable adult."],
["Development: Adversity and deprivation","Risk and resilience","A 9-year-old boy in a chaotic, violent home is doing well at school. Which single factor is most often found in resilient children like him?","A warm, stable relationship with at least one caring adult","One secure relationship buffers stress and is the most consistent protective factor."],
/* ---------- Development: Moral and gender development ---------- */
["Development: Moral and gender development","Piaget's moral development","What is Piaget's heteronomous morality?","Rules are fixed and made by authority, and acts are judged by their results","Seen in younger children up to about 9-10 years. Breaking more cups by accident seems worse than breaking one on purpose."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Piaget's moral development","What is Piaget's autonomous morality?","Rules can be changed by agreement, and acts are judged by intentions","It develops from about 10 years as children mix with peers as equals."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Piaget's moral development","What is immanent justice?","The belief that wrongdoing is always punished automatically","For example, falling over because you lied. It belongs to heteronomous morality."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Kohlberg's moral development","How did Kohlberg study moral reasoning?","With moral dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma","He scored the reasons people gave, not their yes or no answer."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Kohlberg's moral development","Name Kohlberg's three levels of moral development.","Pre-conventional, conventional, post-conventional","Each level has two stages, giving six in all."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Kohlberg's moral development","What guides moral choice at Kohlberg's pre-conventional level?","Avoiding punishment and getting rewards","Stage 1 is punishment and obedience; stage 2 is self-interest and fair exchange. It lasts up to about 9-10 years."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Kohlberg's moral development","An 8-year-old boy says you must obey the teacher's rules because otherwise you get a detention. Which Kohlberg level is he at?","Pre-conventional","Right and wrong depend on punishment, not on shared values."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Kohlberg's moral development","What are the two stages of Kohlberg's conventional level?","Good boy/good girl (seeking approval) and law and order (authority orientation)","Morality is about meeting others' expectations and keeping social rules."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Kohlberg's moral development","A 12-year-old girl says she does the right thing because it pleases her parents and teachers. Which Kohlberg stage is this?","Good boy/good girl orientation (conventional level)","Behaviour is judged by whether it wins approval from others."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Kohlberg's moral development","What are the two stages of Kohlberg's post-conventional level?","Social contract and universal ethical principles","Laws are seen as agreements that can change, and conscience follows universal principles such as justice."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Kohlberg's moral development","Does everyone reach Kohlberg's post-conventional level?","No, only a minority (about 10 to 15%) of adults","Most adults reason at the conventional level. Universal ethical principles are rarely reached."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Kohlberg's moral development","What was Carol Gilligan's main criticism of Kohlberg?","His theory was male-biased and ignored an ethic of care","Kohlberg stressed justice. Gilligan argued many women reason from care and relationships, which his scoring rated lower."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Gender development","What is the difference between gender identity and gender role?","Gender identity is the inner sense of one's gender; gender role is the behaviour society expects of that gender","Sexual orientation is a separate concept: who one is attracted to."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Gender development","A 3-year-old boy is upset when a stranger calls him a girl. Which capacity has developed?","Gender identity","He knows and values being a boy. Gender labelling is usually in place by about 2 to 3 years."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Gender development","Give the three steps of Kohlberg's cognitive theory of gender development with ages.","Gender identity (about 2), gender stability (about 4), gender constancy (about 6 to 7)","The child first labels gender, then knows it lasts over time, then knows it does not change with clothes or hair."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Gender development","How does social learning theory explain gender development?","Children learn gender roles by watching models and being rewarded or punished","Bandura's theory stresses modelling of parents, peers and media. Archetypes play no part in it."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Gender development","What does gender schema theory propose?","Children build mental categories about gender and use them to guide what they notice and do","Described by Martin and Halverson, and by Bem. It combines cognitive and social views."],
["Development: Moral and gender development","Gender development","Where is gender incongruence placed in ICD-11?","In the chapter on conditions related to sexual health, not mental disorders","This change aims to reduce stigma while keeping access to care."],
/* ---------- Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing ---------- */
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Puberty","What is the average age of puberty onset in UK boys and girls?","About 11 to 12 years in boys; about a year earlier in girls","Girls usually start with breast buds; boys with testicular enlargement."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Puberty","Below what ages is puberty considered precocious?","Before 8 years in girls and 9 years in boys","Earlier signs need assessment for a hormonal or central cause."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Puberty","What is the first sign of puberty in girls and in boys?","Girls: breast budding; boys: testicular enlargement","Menarche comes later in girls, usually about 2 years after breast budding."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Puberty","Which social factors can bring puberty forward?","Family stress, such as family break-up and father absence","Obesity also brings it earlier. The age of menarche has fallen over the last century."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Puberty","A 10-year-old girl starts puberty much earlier than her classmates. Which mental health risks are raised for her?","Depression, eating problems, substance use and early sexual activity","Early-maturing girls face pressures they are not ready for. Effects in boys are more mixed."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Adolescence","Is adolescent turmoil and conflict with parents the norm?","No, most adolescents get on reasonably well with their parents","Rutter's Isle of Wight study found inner misery was common but open conflict was not. Marked turmoil may signal a disorder."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Adolescence","What are Marcia's four identity statuses?","Diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, achievement","They depend on whether the young person has explored options and made commitments."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Adolescence","What is foreclosure in Marcia's model?","Commitment without exploration, often taking on parents' values","Moratorium is exploration without commitment; achievement is commitment after exploration."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Adolescence","What are the imaginary audience and personal fable?","Forms of adolescent egocentrism described by Elkind","The teenager feels constantly watched and believes they are unique and invulnerable, which feeds risk-taking."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Adolescence","Why are adolescents prone to risk-taking, in brain terms?","Reward systems mature early but prefrontal control matures later, into the mid-20s","This gap means strong drive for reward with weaker brakes, especially with peers present."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Adolescence","How are negative transitions to adulthood affected in societies that use initiation ceremonies?","They are less common","Clear rites of passage mark a defined adult role and reduce uncertainty and risk-taking."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Adulthood","When does the midlife transition occur in Levinson's model?","About 40 to 45 years","People review their goals and limits. Only a minority have a true crisis."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Adulthood","A 55-year-old woman feels low and lonely after her youngest daughter leaves home for university. What is this called?","Empty nest syndrome (distress)","It is most likely when parenting has been the main role. Most parents feel relief rather than distress."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Adulthood","What is downshifting?","Giving up a high-pressure, well-paid job for a simpler, more fulfilling life","It is a voluntary change, often in midlife, not an illness."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Ageing","What is disengagement theory of ageing?","Older people and society withdraw from each other by mutual consent","Cumming and Henry proposed it. Critics say withdrawal is neither universal nor healthy."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Ageing","Which ageing theory says ageist society forces older people out of social roles, rather than mutual withdrawal?","Social reengagement theory (activity loss theory)","Loss of roles is imposed by society, not chosen by the person."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Ageing","What does activity theory of ageing propose?","Older people stay happiest by keeping up activities and roles, or replacing lost ones","It opposes disengagement theory. Continuity theory adds that people keep their earlier habits and style."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Ageing","Which type of intelligence is best kept in old age?","Crystallised intelligence (knowledge and vocabulary)","Fluid intelligence and processing speed decline from early adulthood. Crystallised ability holds up into the 60s and 70s."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Ageing","Which cognitive function is least affected by working memory problems?","Long-term recall of stored memories","Executive tasks, such as error correction and planning, depend heavily on working memory. Old stored memories do not."],
["Development: Adolescence, adulthood and ageing","Ageing","How does sleep change in healthy old age?","Less deep slow-wave sleep, more waking at night and earlier sleep and waking times","Total sleep need changes little, but sleep becomes lighter and more broken."],
/* ---------- Development: Grief and bereavement ---------- */
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Models of grief","Who published the classic 1944 study of acute grief, after the Cocoanut Grove fire?","Erich Lindemann","He described shock, intense sadness, protest and then resolution, usually within a year."],
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Models of grief","In Lindemann's account of acute grief, how does anger show itself after the initial shock?","As protest against the loss","Protest is part of normal grief. Bargaining, by contrast, is a Kubler-Ross stage described in dying patients."],
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Models of grief","Name the stages of grief described by Bowlby and Parkes.","Numbness, yearning and searching, disorganisation and despair, reorganisation","These overlap and do not run in a strict order."],
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Models of grief","A 68-year-old woman widowed a month ago sleeps and eats poorly, longs for her husband, and hears his voice at night. Which phase of grief is this?","The second phase: yearning and acute distress","Preoccupation, pining and sensing the dead person are normal at this stage. It is not psychosis."],
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Models of grief","Is it normal for a bereaved person to see or hear the person who died?","Yes, such experiences are common in normal grief","Up to about half of widowed people report them. They are usually comforting and not a sign of mental illness."],
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Models of grief","Name Kubler-Ross's five stages of facing death.","Denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance","She described them in dying patients. They are not fixed steps and do not always occur in order."],
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Models of grief","What does the dual process model of grief describe?","Swinging between facing the loss and rebuilding life","Stroebe and Schut called these loss-oriented and restoration-oriented coping. Oscillation is healthy."],
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Models of grief","Name Worden's four tasks of mourning.","Accept the loss, work through the pain, adjust to life without the person, find a lasting bond while moving on","Worden saw mourning as active work rather than passive stages."],
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Complicated grief","What is the minimum time since the loss for prolonged grief disorder in ICD-11?","At least 6 months","Grief must clearly exceed social and cultural norms and cause marked impairment. DSM-5-TR uses 12 months for adults."],
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Complicated grief","Give risk factors for prolonged or complicated grief.","Sudden or violent death, death of a child, a dependent or ambivalent relationship, poor support, past losses","These make the loss harder to process and accept."],
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Complicated grief","Which group shows a clear rise in mortality after bereavement?","Widowers, especially in the first 6 months","Parkes described this broken-heart effect. Heart disease is a major cause."],
["Development: Grief and bereavement","Complicated grief","By what age do most children understand that death is permanent and universal?","About 5 to 7 years","Younger children often think death is reversible, like sleep."],
/* ---------- Development: Pregnancy and perinatal ---------- */
["Development: Pregnancy and perinatal","Perinatal mood","When do the baby blues usually start and end?","Start day 3 to 5 after birth; settle by about 10 to 14 days","They affect about half or more of mothers. Tearfulness and lability are typical. No treatment beyond support is needed."],
["Development: Pregnancy and perinatal","Perinatal mood","About how common is postnatal depression?","About 10 to 15% of mothers","It is similar in rate to depression at other times, but it affects the developing infant too."],
["Development: Pregnancy and perinatal","Perinatal mood","How common is postpartum psychosis and when does it usually begin?","About 1 to 2 per 1000 births; usually within the first 2 weeks","It is a psychiatric emergency, usually needing admission to a mother and baby unit."],
["Development: Pregnancy and perinatal","Perinatal mood","A 28-year-old woman with bipolar disorder, 34 weeks pregnant, asks her GP about her risk after birth. What is the main risk?","Postpartum psychosis (about 1 in 4 to 1 in 5 risk)","Bipolar disorder is the strongest risk factor. A perinatal mental health team should plan care before birth."],
["Development: Pregnancy and perinatal","Perinatal mood","Which brief questions does NICE advise for depression screening in pregnancy and after birth?","The two Whooley questions (low mood and loss of interest in the past month)","A positive answer leads to fuller assessment, for example with the EPDS or PHQ-9."],
["Development: Pregnancy and perinatal","Effects on the child","How can maternal postnatal depression affect the child?","Insecure attachment, poorer cognitive development and more behaviour problems","Reduced sensitivity and responsiveness in the mother is the likely route. Treating the mother helps the child."],
["Development: Pregnancy and perinatal","Effects on the child","What does Tronick's still-face experiment show?","Infants become distressed and withdrawn when a parent stops responding","It shows infants actively expect contingent responses. It models the impact of a depressed, unresponsive carer."],
["Development: Pregnancy and perinatal","Effects on the child","What is linked with high maternal anxiety or stress during pregnancy?","More emotional and behavioural problems in the child","Cohort studies such as ALSPAC show this link, possibly via fetal stress hormone exposure."],
["Development: Pregnancy and perinatal","Effects on the child","What is primary maternal preoccupation?","Winnicott's term for the mother's intense focus on the baby in late pregnancy and early weeks","This heightened sensitivity helps her meet the newborn's needs."],
["Development: Pregnancy and perinatal","Pregnancy experiences","What is couvade syndrome?","An expectant father has pregnancy-like symptoms","Nausea, weight gain or abdominal pain occur without physical cause. Symptoms usually resolve after birth."],
["Development: Pregnancy and perinatal","Pregnancy experiences","A 32-year-old woman at her booking appointment has an intense fear of childbirth and wants to avoid it. What is this called?","Tokophobia","It may be primary or follow a traumatic birth. Referral to a perinatal mental health service is advised."],
]);
